    "submission": "submission-map-c11.json",
    "summary": "Turn 74 recorded the confirmed startup-path defect at its coordinate in the map.\n\nState on entry. C11-replica-startup-avoids-external-probe is confirmed in scope: the local-address helper discards its dial error and dereferences the failed connection, and the trusted runner reproduced the nil-pointer panic in an environment without a route to the fixed probe address — so constructing a replica, which evaluates that helper inline, cannot complete there.\n\nMap update (accepted base version 15, one clarified surface). The replica/defs/latency.go surface now describes both roles of the file: injecting per-peer delays for peer delivery and client proposals, and providing the local-address helper that replica construction evaluates as an argument to the latency table, with the confirmed consequence that an unreachable probe address prevents construction at all. Its sources now include the helper's declaration, the constructor's call site and the listener that consumes the delays; the map_changes entry carries the rationale and a preserves explanation, so the surface's role as scheduling infrastructure and its delay description are unchanged, and no requirement, binding, edge, identity or claim moved.\n\nFrontier. Eleven claim families are settled: nine confirmed violations or consequences — C1 (recovery divergence, now on a driven proposal and commit history, with its six artifacts), C2 (durable-record layout), C3 (applied-state divergence), C4 (NOOP-commit crash), C6 (promoted-leader variant), C7 (dead accept-rejection path), C9 (recorder row, two scenarios), C10 (codec truncation, stream shift and receiver decode panic), C11 (startup probe) — and two bounded no-violation protections (C5 owner-alive re-proposal, C8 executor timeout enqueue). Two candidates remain paused with scoped resume conditions, one issue remains structurally unresolvable on a superseded artifact, and the unread items are named: whether the project's deployments reach the probe address, the reader-side reaction to a displaced stream, and whether the client caps the command size it builds. No requirement, premise or earlier observation was weakened."
